The <dual space> is the <vector space> of all <linear functionals> on $V$:
$$
\boxed{V'=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb C}(V,\mathbb C)}.
$$
Addition and <scalar multiplication> are defined pointwise. The <dual map> sends an output measurement back along $T$:
$$
\boxed{T':V'\to V',\qquad T'(\varphi)=\varphi\circ T,\qquad (T'\varphi)(v)=\varphi(Tv)}.
$$
The <function composition> of these <linear maps> is linear, and $T'$ is linear in $\varphi$. This is the algebraic <dual map>, so its definition involves no <complex conjugation> or <inner product>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a <vector subspace> $U\subseteq V$, its <annihilator of a vector subspace> is
$$
\boxed{U^0=\{\varphi\in V':\varphi(u)=0\text{ for every }u\in U\}}.
$$
Let $\dim U=k$ and $\dim V=n$. Extend a <basis> $u_1,\ldots,u_k$ of $U$ to a <basis> $u_1,\ldots,u_n$ of $V$, and let $\varepsilon^1,\ldots,\varepsilon^n$ be the <dual basis>. Every <linear functional> has the unique expression $\varphi=\sum_{j=1}^n\varphi(u_j)\varepsilon^j$. It belongs to $U^0$ precisely when its first $k$ coefficients vanish. Consequently
$$
U^0=\operatorname{span}\{\varepsilon^{k+1},\ldots,\varepsilon^n\},\qquad
\boxed{\dim U+\dim U^0=\dim V}.
$$
The listed <dual basis> elements are <linearly independent>, so counting them proves the formula, including $U=0$ and $U=V$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Suppose $U$ is an <invariant subspace> for $T$. For $\varphi\in U^0$ and $u\in U$, the <dual map> satisfies
$$
(T'\varphi)(u)=\varphi(Tu)=0,
$$
because $Tu\in U$ and the <annihilator of a vector subspace> consists of the <linear functionals> vanishing there. Thus
$$
\boxed{T'(U^0)\subseteq U^0}.
$$
This is the correspondence between <invariant subspaces and annihilators>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

We prove by <mathematical induction> on $n=\dim V$ that every <linear map> on an $n$-dimensional <complex vector space> admits both triangular forms. For $n=0$ or $1$ this is immediate. When $n>1$, the <fundamental theorem of algebra> supplies an <eigenvector> $v$; its <span> $U=\langle v\rangle$ is an <invariant subspace>.

By parts (ii) and (iii), $U^0$ has <dimension> $n-1$ and is an <invariant subspace> of the <dual map> $T'$. Apply the induction hypothesis to $T'|_{U^0}$ to choose a <basis> $\varphi_1,\ldots,\varphi_{n-1}$ with an <upper triangular matrix>. Extend it by a <linear functional> $\varphi_n$ satisfying $\varphi_n(v)=1$. Such a functional exists by <basis extension>, and cannot belong to $U^0$. In this <basis> of $V'$, the <matrix> of $T'$ has the form
$$
C=\begin{pmatrix}B&b\\0&\lambda\end{pmatrix},
$$
where $B$ is an <upper triangular matrix>; here the last diagonal entry follows by evaluating $T'\varphi_n$ at $v$, where $Tv=\lambda v$.

There is a <basis> $e_1,\ldots,e_n$ of $V$ with $\varphi_i(e_j)=\delta_{ij}$, since the <dual basis> construction gives a finite-dimensional <bidual evaluation map> isomorphism. In particular $e_n=v$. The <matrix transpose> relationship for a <dual map> gives
$$
[T]_{(e_1,\ldots,e_n)}=C^T,
$$
a <lower triangular matrix>. Reversing this <basis> turns a <lower triangular matrix> into an <upper triangular matrix>. If $R$ is the <permutation matrix> reversing the order, we have proved
$$
\boxed{[T]_{(e_1,\ldots,e_n)}=C^T\text{ lower triangular},\qquad
[T]_{(e_n,\ldots,e_1)}=RC^TR\text{ upper triangular}}.
$$
This completes the induction by <triangularization using dual spaces>.

