= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $K_i=\ker T^i$, with $K_0=\{0\}$. The <minimal polynomial> $x^m$ means $T^m=0$ and $T^{m-1}\ne0$. Choose $w$ with $T^{m-1}w\ne0$. For each $0\leq i<m$, set $w_i=T^{m-1-i}w$. Then
$$
T^{i+1}w_i=0,\qquad T^iw_i=T^{m-1}w\ne0.
$$
Thus $w_i\in K_{i+1}\setminus K_i$. Also $K_i\subseteq K_{i+1}$ because applying $T$ to zero still gives zero. Therefore the <kernel dimensions of powers of a nilpotent map> increase strictly until the <nilpotency index>:
$$
\boxed{\{0\}=K_0\subsetneq K_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq K_m=V}.
$$
The same reasoning covers $m=1$, when the <nilpotent linear map> is zero on a nonzero <vector space>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $d_i=\dim K_i$, including $d_0=0$. The <restriction> of $T^i$ to $K_{i+1}$ has <kernel> $K_i$, so the <rank-nullity theorem> gives
$$
\dim T^i(K_{i+1})=d_{i+1}-d_i.
$$
Likewise $T^{i-1}|_{K_i}$ has <kernel> $K_{i-1}$ and hence image <dimension> $d_i-d_{i-1}$. But $T(K_{i+1})\subseteq K_i$, which implies
$$
T^i(K_{i+1})=T^{i-1}(T(K_{i+1}))\subseteq T^{i-1}(K_i).
$$
Comparing <dimensions> yields the required concavity of the <kernel dimensions of powers of a nilpotent map>:
$$
\boxed{d_{i+1}-d_i\leq d_i-d_{i-1},\qquad 2d_i\geq d_{i-1}+d_{i+1}\quad(1\leq i<m)}.
$$

= b
{parent=2}
