{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <adjoint operator> of $T:V\to V$ is a <linear map> $T^*:V\to V$ satisfying
$$
\boxed{\langle Tu,w\rangle=\langle u,T^*w\rangle\qquad\text{for all }u,w\in V}.
$$
This identity transfers $T$ from the first input of the <inner product> to the second.

= ii
{parent=a}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $R$ and $S$ both satisfy the defining identity of an <adjoint operator>, then
$$
\langle u,(R-S)w\rangle=\langle Tu,w\rangle-\langle Tu,w\rangle=0
$$
for every $u,w\in V$. Choosing $u=(R-S)w$ gives $\|(R-S)w\|^2=0$. The <positive definiteness> of the <inner product> forces $(R-S)w=0$ for every $w$, proving
$$
\boxed{R=S}.
$$

