= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <normal operator> $T$, the <adjoint operator> identity and $T^*T=TT^*$ give
$$
\|Tv\|^2=\langle v,T^*Tv\rangle
=\langle v,TT^*v\rangle
=\|T^*v\|^2.
$$
For the last equality, $\langle TT^*v,v\rangle=\langle T^*v,T^*v\rangle$ and <complex conjugation> allow reversal of the two arguments. Taking the nonnegative square roots proves
$$
\boxed{\|Tv\|=\|T^*v\|\quad(v\in V)}.
$$
In particular the two <kernels> coincide.

= ii
{parent=b}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $w=Tv$. If $T^2v=0$, then $Tw=0$, and part (i) gives $\|T^*w\|=\|Tw\|=0$. Thus $T^*w=0$ and
$$
\|w\|^2=\langle Tv,w\rangle=\langle v,T^*w\rangle=0.
$$
The <positive definiteness> of the <inner product> gives $w=0$. Since the reverse <kernel> inclusion always holds, we obtain
$$
\boxed{T^2v=0\Longrightarrow Tv=0,\qquad \ker T^2=\ker T}.
$$
This is <kernel stabilization for a normal operator>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a <polynomial> $f(x)=\sum_{j=0}^d a_jx^j$, the defining identity and uniqueness of the <adjoint operator> give
$$
f(T)^*=\sum_{j=0}^d\overline{a_j}(T^*)^j.
$$
Indeed, the <adjoint operator> of a sum is the sum of the <adjoint operators>, the adjoint of $aT$ is $\bar aT^*$, and the adjoint of a product $AB$ is $B^*A^*$. Since the <normal operator> $T$ commutes with $T^*$, each power of $T$ commutes with each power of $T^*$. Multiplying the two finite sums therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f(T)f(T)^*=f(T)^*f(T)}.
$$
Hence <polynomials of a normal operator are normal>, including constant <polynomials>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Suppose some exponent $q_j\geq2$. Define
$$
h(x)=\frac{m_T(x)}{f_j(x)^2},\qquad N=f_j(T).
$$
Part (iii) makes $N$ a <normal operator>. For every $v\in V$, put $w=h(T)v$. The <minimal polynomial> identity gives
$$
N^2w=f_j(T)^2h(T)v=m_T(T)v=0.
$$
Part (ii), applied to $N$, forces $Nw=0$. As this holds for every $v$,
$$
0=Nh(T)=\left(\frac{m_T}{f_j}\right)(T).
$$
This contradicts minimality: $m_T/f_j$ is a <monic polynomial> of smaller degree annihilating $T$. Therefore the <minimal polynomial of a normal operator> is a <square-free polynomial> over either of the given <fields>:
$$
\boxed{q_1=\cdots=q_r=1,\qquad m_T=f_1\cdots f_r}.
$$
This argument does not require the <irreducible polynomials> to be linear, so it also covers the real case.

