Take $N=T-\lambda I$. It is a <polynomial> in the <normal operator> $T$, so part (b)(iii) makes $N$ a <normal operator>. Also $N^*=T^*-\bar\lambda I$. If $Tv=\lambda v$, part (b)(i), applied to $N$, gives
$$
\|(T^*-\bar\lambda I)v\|=\|N^*v\|=\|Nv\|=0.
$$
The <norm> vanishes only at the zero <vector>, so
$$
\boxed{T^*v=\bar\lambda v}.
$$
This is the <adjoint eigenvector identity for a normal matrix> expressed independently of <coordinates>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $u\in\ker(T-\lambda I)$ and $w\in\ker(T-\mu I)$, with $\lambda\ne\mu$. By part (i) and the <adjoint operator> identity,
$$
\lambda\langle u,w\rangle=\langle Tu,w\rangle
=\langle u,T^*w\rangle
=\langle u,\bar\mu w\rangle
=\mu\langle u,w\rangle.
$$
The last step uses conjugate-linearity in the second argument of the <inner product>. Therefore $(\lambda-\mu)\langle u,w\rangle=0$, proving the <orthogonality> of the two <eigenspaces>:
$$
\boxed{\ker(T-\lambda I)\perp\ker(T-\mu I)\quad(\lambda\ne\mu)}.
$$

= iii
{parent=c}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Over the <complex numbers>, the <fundamental theorem of algebra> makes every <irreducible polynomial> factor $f_j(x)$ equal to $x-\lambda_j$. Part (b)(iv) gives
$$
m_T(x)=\prod_{j=1}^r(x-\lambda_j)
$$
with distinct <eigenvalues>. The <primary decomposition theorem for linear maps> consequently expresses $V$ as the <direct sum> of the actual <eigenspaces> $E_{\lambda_j}=\ker(T-\lambda_jI)$, with no higher <generalized eigenspaces> needed. Part (ii) makes these summands mutually <orthogonal>.

Apply the <Gram-Schmidt process> to a <basis> within each <eigenspace>. Its <linear combinations> stay within that <eigenspace>, so all resulting <vectors> are still <eigenvectors>. Their union is <orthonormal> by part (ii) and spans $V$ by the <primary decomposition theorem for linear maps>. Thus
$$
\boxed{V=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{r} E_{\lambda_j},\qquad
[T]_{\mathcal B}=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1I_{\dim E_{\lambda_1}},\ldots,\lambda_rI_{\dim E_{\lambda_r}})}
$$
for an <orthonormal basis> $\mathcal B$ of <eigenvectors>. This proves the finite-dimensional complex <spectral theorem for normal operators> by the requested decomposition argument.
